                <div data-article-body="true" data-track-component="article body" class="c-article-body">

                    
                    <section aria-labelledby="Abs1" data-title="Abstract" lang="en"><div class="c-article-section" id="Abs1-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Abs1">Abstract</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Abs1-content"><p>Pakistan is currently facing severe environmental challenges, higher country risk (political, economic, and financial), and an increase in the size of its shadow economy. Financial development can be used to reduce shadow economic activities and environmental degradation. However, the country’s risk restricts the efforts to reduce environmental degradation and promote financial development. This motivates us to examine the nexus between country risk and CO<sub>2</sub> emissions. Therefore, this study explores the impact of financial development, shadow economy and country risk on environmental degradation in Pakistan, using data from 1990 to 2020. The study utilizes the conventional ADF, Fourier GLS/ADF unit root test, RALS-FARDL, and Bayer &amp; Hanck (2013) cointegration test. The study utilizes FMOLS, DOLS and CCR to estimate long-run relationships, and the findings indicate that country risk intensifies carbon emissions. It is also found that financial development and economic growth are positively related to carbon emissions, while the shadow economy is negatively related. Moreover, employing the single-frequency Toda &amp; Yamamoto causality test, the study examines the bidirectional causality between carbon emissions and country risk. This study recommends the adoption of environmentally friendly technologies financed by the banking sector to lessen environmental pollution, which is important for sustainable development.</p></div></div></section>

                    

                    
    


                    
                        
                            
